        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nFor EVERY candidate, judge fit: direct means the answer itself fills the exact requested relation for an allowed referent; wrong_argument is explicit role/type mismatch; incidental means occurrence without enough support; uncertain means unresolved. Select support only for an original segment positively assigning this candidate to the requested relation; otherwise empty. Select counter only for actual counterevidence, not mere absence. conflict=same_referent only for incompatible claims for the same entity/attribute/relevant scope, not legitimate namesakes or complementary facts. entity labels are provisional groups, not truth. scope=excluded only for an explicit question constraint, with constraint selecting a literal numeric phrase from the question; otherwise allowed/unknown. observed_slot describes the answer's ACTUAL role, not the role wanted by the question. Mere disagreement does not make a source false. Fill all opaque keys.\nConservative audit with a granularity check. Verify each candidate against ALL original segments. A directly stated second artist, second namesake birth date or second sport is direct even if it is not the first candidate. Positive statement goes in support; counter is only actual incompatible evidence for that exact referent, never a support sentence relabeled negative. Equivalent date forms are not competing facts. Do not reject a date merely because wording or calendar notation differs. Likewise do not admit a loose synonym, associated person or more detailed date as a NEW independent answer. Respect the exact requested answer type; cities cannot answer when-born. Keep unresolved factual reliability separate from role errors.\nInput: {\"question\": \"Who directed the film \\\"Yellow Ticket\\\"?\", \"slot_hint\": \"person\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 199, \"text\": \"The Yellow Ticket The Yellow Ticket is a 1931 pre-Code American drama film based on the 1914 play of the same name by Michael Morton, produced by the Fox Film Corporation and directed by Raoul Walsh.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 200, \"end\": 275, \"text\": \"The film starred Elissa Landi, Lionel Barrymore and featured Boris Karloff.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 276, \"end\": 331, \"text\": \"The film is also noted for its brief glimpse of nudity.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 332, \"end\": 444, \"text\": \"The play was produced in 1914 on Broadway; it starred Lionel's younger brother John Barrymore and Florence Reed.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 445, \"end\": 503, \"text\": \"The stage version played between January 20 and June 1914.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"start\": 504, \"end\": 584, \"text\": \"The play was previously made into an American film \\\"The Yellow Passport\\\" in 1916\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 136, \"text\": \"The Yellow Ticket (1918 film) The Yellow Ticket is a 1918 American silent drama film directed by William Parke and starring Fannie Ward.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 137, \"end\": 200, \"text\": \"It is based on Michael Morton's 1914 play, \\\"The Yellow Ticket\\\".\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 201, \"end\": 392, \"text\": \"Anna Mirrel, a young Jewish girl in Czarist Russia, is forced to pretend to be a prostitute to obtain a passport (a \\\"yellow ticket\\\") in order to visit her father, whom she believes to be ill.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 393, \"end\": 416, \"text\": \"When she arrives in St.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 417, \"end\": 472, \"text\": \"Petersburg, she learns that her father has been killed.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"start\": 473, \"end\": 568, \"text\": \"She encounters a young journalist and tells him of the crimes the state perpetrates against its\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Raoul Walsh\", \"scope\": \"The film \\\"Yellow Ticket\\\"\", \"claim\": \"Raoul Walsh directed the film \\\"Yellow Ticket\\\".\", \"answer\": \"Raoul Walsh\", \"quote\": \"The Yellow Ticket The Yellow Ticket is a 1931 pre-Code American drama film based on the 1914 play of the same name by Michael Morton, produced by the Fox Film Corporation and directed by Raoul Walsh.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0108-d1a5f809cf6e:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"film director\", \"scope\": \"The film \\\"Yellow Ticket\\\" (1918)\", \"claim\": \"William Parke directed the film \\\"Yellow Ticket\\\".\", \"answer\": \"William Parke\", \"quote\": \"The Yellow Ticket (1918 film) The Yellow Ticket is a 1918 American silent drama film directed by William Parke and starring Fannie Ward.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"Raoul Walsh\", \"William Parke\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"William Parke\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"Raoul Walsh\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
